title 03 | Socket APIs, TCP, TLS, HTTP, and closure [overview stages 3-4, 6, 8]
participant "Client HTTP\napplication" as C #DBEAFE
participant "Client TLS\nlibrary" as CT #FCE7F3
participant "Client socket API\n+ OS TCP" as CS #EDE9FE
participant "Server socket API\n+ OS TCP" as SS #EDE9FE
participant "Server TLS\nlibrary" as ST #FCE7F3
participant "Server HTTP\napplication" as S #DBEAFE

note over C,S
  READ FIRST: local arrows are API calls; TCP arrows are wire exchanges.
  TLS handshake arrows between libraries are logical exchanges:
  they ALL traverse sockets -> TCP -> IP -> link -> physical [04].
  This is a blocking-style teaching model; real servers often use event loops.
  Baseline: full TLS 1.3 handshake, no resumption, no client certificate.
end note

== 3a | Server establishes a listening endpoint ==
S -> SS: socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM)
SS --> S: Listening socket descriptor
S -> SS: bind(local-address, 443); listen(backlog)
S -> SS: accept(): wait for a connection
note over SS,S #EDE9FE
  Listening socket accepts connections.
  Each accepted socket represents one connected TCP endpoint;
  the listener remains available for other clients.
end note

== 3b | Client establishes a TCP connection ==
C -> CS: socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM)
CS --> C: Socket descriptor
C -> CS: connect(203.0.113.20, 443)
CS -> CS: Choose local address + ephemeral port 51514\nCreate connection state; determine route / next hop
CS -> SS: SYN: Seq=x; MSS / window options\nVia all lower layers; optional NAT [04]
SS --> CS: SYN + ACK: Seq=y, Ack=x+1
CS -> SS: ACK: Seq=x+1, Ack=y+1
CS --> C: connect() completes successfully
SS --> S: accept() returns connected socket + peer endpoint
note over CS,SS #EDE9FE
  Connection identity: source IP/port + destination IP/port, within TCP.
  Client sees 192.168.1.10:51514 -> 203.0.113.20:443.
  With example NAT, server sees peer 198.51.100.10:62000.
  TCP exposes a byte stream; SYN consumes one sequence number.
end note

== 4 | TLS 1.3 secures this connection ==
C -> CT: Attach connected socket; start TLS client handshake
S -> ST: Attach accepted socket; start TLS server handshake
CT -> ST: ClientHello (unencrypted in this baseline)\nversions, cipher suites, key share;\nSNI=www.example.com; ALPN offers
ST --> CT: ServerHello: selected version/cipher + key share
CT -> CT: Derive handshake keys from\nephemeral shared secret + transcript
ST -> ST: Derive handshake keys
ST --> CT: EncryptedExtensions (selected ALPN: http/1.1)\nCertificate; CertificateVerify; Finished\nProtected with server handshake keys
CT -> CT: Validate trusted certificate chain, validity, hostname;\nverify CertificateVerify signature and Finished
note over CT,ST #FCE7F3
  Certificate validation failure aborts the secure connection.
  Shared traffic secrets are derived; a session key is not simply sent.
  Use separate directional application traffic keys.
  No ECH is illustrated: ordinary SNI is visible in ClientHello.
  HTTP Host/path/body are protected after encryption.
end note
CT -> ST: Client Finished, protected with client handshake keys
ST -> ST: Verify client Finished
CT --> C: Secure connection ready
ST --> S: Secure connection ready

== 5-6 | Encrypted HTTP request and server processing ==
C -> CT: Write HTTP bytes:\nGET /hello HTTP/1.1\nHost: www.example.com
CT -> CT: Frame TLS records; AEAD encrypt + authenticate
CT -> CS: send()/write() encrypted bytes to socket
CS -> SS: TCP byte stream carried in IP packets,\nlink frames, physical signals [04]
SS --> ST: recv()/read() supplies encrypted stream bytes
ST -> ST: Assemble records; verify tag; decrypt
ST --> S: Deliver plaintext HTTP bytes
S -> S: Parse HTTP framing; route /hello;\nrun handler; prepare response

== 7 | Response uses the same full-duplex connection ==
S -> ST: HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Length: 5; body: Hello
ST -> ST: Encrypt/authenticate response records
ST -> SS: send()/write() encrypted bytes
SS --> CS: Response byte stream; lower-layer path [04]
CS --> CT: recv()/read() encrypted bytes
CT -> CT: Authenticate/decrypt; reconstruct plaintext
CT --> C: HTTP response bytes
C -> C: Parse status, headers, body framing; consume Hello
note over C,S
  Libraries may buffer and perform partial reads/writes.
  One HTTP message can span many TLS records and TCP segments.
  HTTP/2 can multiplex streams on one TLS/TCP connection;
  it uses HTTP/2 framing after ALPN selects h2.
end note

== 8 | Reuse, or one possible orderly shutdown ==
alt Keep connection for more requests
  C -> C: Reuse the established connection\nSkip DNS/TCP/TLS setup while applicable
else Client initiates graceful shutdown
  C -> CT: Finish application work; initiate TLS shutdown
  CT -> ST: Encrypted close_notify alert
  ST --> CT: Encrypted close_notify alert\nExample peer responds orderly
  C -> CS: close() / shutdown write side after TLS shutdown
  CS -> SS: FIN + ACK (after prior data)
  SS --> CS: ACK
  S -> SS: close() / shutdown write side
  SS --> CS: FIN + ACK
  CS -> SS: ACK
  CS -> CS: Active closer enters TIME_WAIT\nRetire connection state after wait
end
note over CS,SS
  Each TCP direction closes independently; ACK/FIN can be combined.
  Abrupt resets and timeouts follow different paths [05].
  Exact TLS shutdown API behavior depends on the library.
end note
legend left
  |= Color |= Role |
  |<#DBEAFE> Application | HTTP, DNS, application logic |
  |<#FCE7F3> Security | TLS above TCP; SSL is the historical name |
  |<#EDE9FE> Transport | TCP; sockets are a local API boundary |
  |<#FFEDD5> Network | IP addressing and router forwarding |
  |<#DCFCE7> Link | Ethernet / Wi-Fi framing and local delivery |
  |<#FEF3C7> Physical | Electrical, optical, or radio signals |
  Solid arrows: actions / exchanges. Dashed arrows: replies / completions.
  Arrow style does not imply a different delivery guarantee.
endlegend

@enduml
